  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1103 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nClouds were starting to break up, and clearing will continue \nthrough the rest of the day. Cold/dry advection will continue \nthrough tonight, leading to the season's first freeze across most \nif not all of the plains and I-25 Corridor. That's despite a light\nbreeze that will remain over the eastern plains. We expect \novernight lows to be mostly in the mid to upper 20s across the \nplains, with upper 20s and lower 30s along the I-25 Corridor \n(warmest over the western Denver suburbs). \n\nDespite a return to full sunshine on Friday, temperatures will\nremain quite chilly and several degrees below normal. High\ntemperatures will stick mostly in the mid to upper 50s over the\nplains. It will be cold again Friday night, with another freeze\nand mid to upper 20s over the low lying areas of the plains, but\nsome warming along the downslope areas of the I-25 Corridor. \n\nThis weekend will turn warm again as a ridge of high pressure\nbuilds across the Central Rockies Saturday, and then warm\nsouthwest flow occurs on the back side of the ridge Sunday. Highs\nshould mostly reach the 70s across the plains. In the mountains, \nwe can't rule out a weak shower or two Sunday afternoon as a touch\nof mid level moisture returns. \n\nSouthwest flow strengthens through the middle of next week as a\ndeep trough of low pressure moves down the Pacific Coast. That\nkeeps us in a warmer than normal pattern, but a chance of showers\nwill return mostly for the high country.  \n\nWe are watching the arrival of the next storm system for the\nlatter portions of the week, when the West Coast trough pushes\neast across the Rockies. Ensemble systems overall keep the \nhighest probabilities (50-70%) of 1\" or more of liquid equivalent \nfrom the northern mountains into southeast Wyoming and the \nNebraska Panhandle, and remain relatively light over the I-25 \nCorridor and plains. However, there are a few outliers that bear \nwatching as the trough does have an opportunity to become \nnegatively tilted. It's just that for now the majority of the \nensembles support the storm track and heavier precipitation swath \nstaying just staying just to our north.",
